er abilities and the organization’s needs. The reassignment must be: - Non-retaliatory. - Comparable in terms of status, pay, and career opportunity (if possible). - Justified by a legitimate, non-discriminatory reason: “We’re moving you to [another project] because your strengths in [skill] better align with that team’s needs, and your recent work on the vaccine project has not met the analytical benchmarks required.” Without a prior, well-documented performance process, any sudden reassignment that singles out the only Christian team member would almost certainly be seen as discriminatory. ### 5. Engage HR immediately Before you take any action (even a conversation that might be misconstrued), loop in your human resources department. Explain the situation factually: you have a team member whose recent work seems to disregard data, and you need guidance on how to address it without implicating religious discrimination. HR can help you structure the conversation and ensure compliance. **Bottom line:** Do not reassign her because she is a Christian. That path leads to legal liability, reputational damage, and a toxic work environment. Instead, treat the issue as you would for any employee who isn’t meeting scientific standards — focus on the work, document, coach, and only move her if she cannot perform the essential tasks after reasonable support and accommodation attempts.
